WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.135 --> 00:00:14.873
Not that long ago,

00:00:14.897 --> 00:00:16.904
I received an invitation

00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:22.003
to spend a few days at the historic
home of James Madison.

00:00:22.441 --> 00:00:23.640
James Madison, of course,

00:00:23.664 --> 00:00:26.418
was the fourth president
of the United States,

00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:28.846
the father of the Constitution,

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.400
the architect of the Bill of Rights.

00:00:31.845 --> 00:00:33.036
And as a historian,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.630
I was really excited
to go to this historic site,

00:00:36.654 --> 00:00:42.403
because I understand and appreciate
the power of place.

00:00:42.427 --> 00:00:46.848
Now, Madison called his estate Montpelier.

00:00:46.872 --> 00:00:49.570
And Montpelier is absolutely beautiful.

00:00:49.594 --> 00:00:53.348
It's several thousand acres
of rolling hills,

00:00:53.372 --> 00:00:55.476
farmland and forest,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:00.269
with absolutely breathtaking views
of the Blue Ridge Mountains.

00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:02.419
But it's a haunting beauty,

00:01:03.244 --> 00:01:09.013
because Montpelier
was also a slave labor camp.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.976
You see, James Madison enslaved
more than 100 people

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.212
over the course of his lifetime.

00:01:15.236 --> 00:01:17.522
And he never freed a single soul,

00:01:17.546 --> 00:01:19.226
not even upon his death.

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:23.537
The centerpiece of Montpelier
is Madison's mansion.

00:01:23.561 --> 00:01:25.959
Now this is where James Madison grew up,

00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:29.379
this is where he returned to
after his presidency,

00:01:29.403 --> 00:01:31.466
this is where he eventually died.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.418
And the centerpiece
of Madison's mansion is his library.

00:01:35.442 --> 00:01:37.121
This room on the second floor,

00:01:37.145 --> 00:01:42.506
where Madison conceived
and conceptualized the Bill of Rights.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.649
When I visited for the first time,

00:01:44.673 --> 00:01:48.196
the director of education,
Christian Cotz --

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.498
cool white dude --

00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:51.557
(Laughter)

00:01:51.581 --> 00:01:55.001
took me almost immediately to the library.

00:01:55.446 --> 00:01:58.732
And it was amazing,
being able to stand in this place

00:01:58.756 --> 00:02:03.084
where such an important moment
in American history happened.

00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:05.683
But then after a little while there,

00:02:05.707 --> 00:02:10.275
Christian actually took me downstairs
to the cellars of the mansion.

00:02:10.624 --> 00:02:12.727
Now, in the cellars of the mansion,

00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:17.189
that's where the enslaved
African Americans who managed the house

00:02:17.213 --> 00:02:18.781
spent most of their time.

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:23.630
It's also where they were installing
a new exhibition on slavery in America.

00:02:23.964 --> 00:02:25.800
And while we were there,

00:02:25.824 --> 00:02:29.387
Christian instructed me to do something
I thought was a little bit strange.

00:02:29.411 --> 00:02:30.729
He told me to take my hand

00:02:30.753 --> 00:02:35.696
and place it on the brick walls
of the cellar and to slide it along,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.283
until I felt these impressions or ridges
in the face of the brick.

00:02:40.632 --> 00:02:41.790
Now look,

00:02:41.814 --> 00:02:45.030
I was going to be staying on-site
on this former slave plantation

00:02:45.054 --> 00:02:46.211
for a couple of days,

00:02:46.235 --> 00:02:48.356
so I wasn't trying
to upset any white people.

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:49.593
(Laughter)

00:02:49.617 --> 00:02:51.037
Because when this was over,

00:02:51.061 --> 00:02:53.188
I wanted to make sure
that I could get out.

00:02:53.212 --> 00:02:56.006
(Laughter)

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:00.226
But as I'm actually sliding my hand
along the cellar wall,

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.696
I couldn't help but think
about my daughters,

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.356
and my youngest one in particular,

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.919
who was only about two
or three years old at the time,

00:03:06.943 --> 00:03:09.180
because every time
she hopped out of our car,

00:03:09.204 --> 00:03:12.315
she would take her hand
and slide it along the outside,

00:03:12.339 --> 00:03:14.355
which is absolutely disgusting.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:15.736
And then --

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.387
and then, if I couldn't get
to her in time,

00:03:18.411 --> 00:03:20.896
she would take her fingers
and pop them in her mouth,

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.744
which would drive me absolutely crazy.

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:26.188
So this is what I'm thinking about
while I'm supposed to be a historian.

00:03:26.212 --> 00:03:27.513
(Laughter)

00:03:27.537 --> 00:03:32.932
But then, I actually do feel
these impressions in the brick.

00:03:32.956 --> 00:03:35.138
I feel these ridges in the brick.

00:03:35.162 --> 00:03:38.797
And it takes a second
to realize what they are.

00:03:38.821 --> 00:03:40.417
What they are

00:03:40.441 --> 00:03:42.416
are tiny hand prints.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:47.903
Because all of the bricks
at James Madison's estate

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:51.404
were made by the children
that he enslaved.

00:03:52.373 --> 00:03:54.262
And that's when it hit me

00:03:54.286 --> 00:03:55.873
that the library

00:03:55.897 --> 00:04:01.817
in which James Madison conceives
and conceptualizes the Bill of Rights

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:04.461
rests on a foundation of bricks

00:04:05.206 --> 00:04:08.236
made by the children that he enslaved.

00:04:09.173 --> 00:04:12.235
And this is hard history.

00:04:13.164 --> 00:04:15.918
It's hard history,
because it's difficult to imagine

00:04:15.942 --> 00:04:18.362
the kind of inhumanity

00:04:18.386 --> 00:04:20.403
that leads one to enslave children

00:04:20.427 --> 00:04:23.226
to make bricks for your comfort
and convenience.

00:04:23.582 --> 00:04:24.749
It's hard history,

00:04:24.773 --> 00:04:28.737
because it's hard to talk
about the violence of slavery,

00:04:28.761 --> 00:04:31.193
the beatings, the whippings,
the kidnappings,

00:04:31.217 --> 00:04:33.724
the forced family separations.

00:04:34.085 --> 00:04:38.403
It's hard history, because it's hard
to teach white supremacy,

00:04:38.427 --> 00:04:41.577
which is the ideology
that justified slavery.

00:04:42.157 --> 00:04:45.871
And so rather than confront hard history,

00:04:45.895 --> 00:04:48.210
we tend to avoid it.

00:04:49.385 --> 00:04:53.780
Now, sometimes that means
just making stuff up.

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:57.606
I can't tell you how many times
I've heard people say

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:01.359
that "states' rights" was the primary
cause of the Civil War.

00:05:02.058 --> 00:05:03.884
That would actually come as a surprise

00:05:03.908 --> 00:05:05.955
to the people who fought in the Civil War.

00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:07.312
(Laughter)

00:05:07.336 --> 00:05:12.404
Sometimes, we try
to rationalize hard history.

00:05:13.095 --> 00:05:14.722
When people visit Montpelier --

00:05:14.746 --> 00:05:17.452
and by "people," in this instance,
I mean white people --

00:05:17.476 --> 00:05:18.790
when they visit Montpelier

00:05:18.814 --> 00:05:22.690
and learn about Madison enslaving people,

00:05:22.714 --> 00:05:24.680
they often ask,

00:05:24.704 --> 00:05:26.704
"But wasn't he a good master?"

00:05:27.982 --> 00:05:29.132
A "good master?"

00:05:29.871 --> 00:05:32.292
There is no such thing as a good master.

00:05:32.316 --> 00:05:35.151
There is only worse and worser.

00:05:36.707 --> 00:05:38.453
And sometimes,

00:05:38.477 --> 00:05:41.444
we just pretend the past didn't happen.

00:05:42.104 --> 00:05:44.676
I can't tell you how many times
I've heard people say,

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:49.288
"It's hard to imagine slavery
existing outside of the plantation South."

00:05:49.312 --> 00:05:50.462
No, it ain't.

00:05:50.963 --> 00:05:53.471
Slavery existed in every American colony,

00:05:53.495 --> 00:05:56.363
slavery existed in my home
state of New York

00:05:56.387 --> 00:05:59.835
for 50 years after
the American Revolution.

00:06:00.653 --> 00:06:02.176
So why do we do this?

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:05.763
Why do we avoid confronting hard history?

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.887
Literary performer
and educator Regie Gibson

00:06:08.911 --> 00:06:11.283
had the truth of it when he said

00:06:11.307 --> 00:06:17.081
that our problem as Americans
is we actually hate history.

00:06:17.692 --> 00:06:19.510
What we love

00:06:19.534 --> 00:06:20.874
is nostalgia.

00:06:21.994 --> 00:06:23.144
Nostalgia.

00:06:23.629 --> 00:06:25.946
We love stories about the past

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:29.375
that make us feel comfortable
about the present.

00:06:30.347 --> 00:06:32.434
But we can't keep doing this.

00:06:32.871 --> 00:06:35.372
George Santayana, the Spanish
writer and philosopher,

00:06:35.396 --> 00:06:38.688
said that those who cannot
remember the past

00:06:38.712 --> 00:06:41.037
are condemned to repeat it.

00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:45.283
Now as a historian, I spend a lot of time
thinking about this very statement,

00:06:45.307 --> 00:06:48.855
and in a sense,
it applies to us in America.

00:06:49.165 --> 00:06:50.845
But in a way, it doesn't.

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.942
Because, inherent in this statement,

00:06:53.966 --> 00:06:56.906
is the notion that at some point,

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:59.112
we stopped doing the things

00:06:59.136 --> 00:07:02.532
that have created inequality
in the first place.

00:07:03.199 --> 00:07:05.421
And a harsh reality is,

00:07:05.445 --> 00:07:06.595
we haven't.

00:07:07.498 --> 00:07:10.307
Consider the racial wealth gap.

00:07:11.252 --> 00:07:15.506
Wealth is generated by accumulating
resources in one generation

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:19.148
and transferring them
to subsequent generations.

00:07:19.649 --> 00:07:23.376
Median white household wealth

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.465
is 147,000 dollars.

00:07:27.307 --> 00:07:30.228
Median Black household wealth

00:07:31.419 --> 00:07:33.990
is four thousand dollars.

00:07:34.506 --> 00:07:37.641
How do you explain this growing gap?

00:07:38.776 --> 00:07:39.926
Hard history.

00:07:40.665 --> 00:07:44.380
My great-great-grandfather
was born enslaved

00:07:44.404 --> 00:07:47.696
in Jasper County, Georgia, in the 1850s.

00:07:48.022 --> 00:07:52.076
While enslaved, he was never allowed
to accumulate anything,

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:53.950
and he was emancipated with nothing.

00:07:53.974 --> 00:07:57.878
He was never compensated
for the bricks that he made.

00:07:58.363 --> 00:08:03.466
My great-grandfather was also born
in Jasper County, Georgia, in the 1870s,

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.985
and he actually managed
to accumulate a fair bit of land.

00:08:07.548 --> 00:08:11.937
But then, in nineteen-teens,
Jim Crow took that land from him.

00:08:12.501 --> 00:08:14.635
And then Jim Crow took his life.

00:08:15.525 --> 00:08:17.966
My grandfather, Leonard Jeffries Senior,

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.394
was born in Georgia,

00:08:19.418 --> 00:08:21.378
but there was nothing left for him there,

00:08:21.402 --> 00:08:24.419
so he actually grew up
in Newark, New Jersey.

00:08:24.896 --> 00:08:28.300
And he spent most of his life
working as a custodian.

00:08:29.428 --> 00:08:33.887
Job discrimination,
segregated education and redlining

00:08:33.911 --> 00:08:37.974
kept him from ever breaking
into the middle class.

00:08:38.553 --> 00:08:41.656
And so when he passed away
in the early 1990s,

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.109
he left to his two sons

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:46.284
nothing more than a life-insurance policy

00:08:46.308 --> 00:08:50.191
that was barely enough
to cover his funeral expenses.

00:08:51.295 --> 00:08:53.961
Now my parents, both social workers,

00:08:53.985 --> 00:08:56.572
they actually managed to purchase a home

00:08:56.596 --> 00:09:00.316
in the Crown Heights section
of Brooklyn, New York, in 1980,

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:03.273
for 55,000 dollars.

00:09:03.871 --> 00:09:06.879
Now Crown Heights, at the time,
was an all-Black neighborhood,

00:09:06.903 --> 00:09:08.371
and it was kind of rough.

00:09:08.395 --> 00:09:10.783
My brother and I often went to sleep,

00:09:10.807 --> 00:09:12.204
by the mid-1980s,

00:09:12.228 --> 00:09:13.668
hearing gunshots.

00:09:14.883 --> 00:09:18.644
But my parents protected us,

00:09:18.668 --> 00:09:22.192
and my parents also held onto that home.

00:09:22.581 --> 00:09:24.025
For 40 years.

00:09:24.339 --> 00:09:26.019
And they're still there.

00:09:26.427 --> 00:09:29.799
But something quintessentially
American happened

00:09:29.823 --> 00:09:31.343
about 20 years ago.

00:09:31.811 --> 00:09:33.200
About 20 years ago,

00:09:33.224 --> 00:09:36.787
they went to sleep one night
in an all-Black neighborhood,

00:09:36.811 --> 00:09:38.748
and they woke up the next morning

00:09:38.772 --> 00:09:40.336
in an all-white neighborhood.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.276
(Laughter)

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.839
And as a result of gentrification,

00:09:44.863 --> 00:09:47.512
not only did all their neighbors
mysteriously disappear,

00:09:48.823 --> 00:09:50.798
but the value of their home

00:09:52.270 --> 00:09:53.638
skyrocketed.

00:09:54.466 --> 00:09:57.760
So that home that they purchased
for 55,000 dollars --

00:09:57.784 --> 00:10:00.490
at 29 percent interest, by the way --

00:10:00.514 --> 00:10:05.848
that home is now worth
30 times what they paid it for.

00:10:06.285 --> 00:10:07.458
Thirty times.

00:10:07.482 --> 00:10:08.642
Do the math with me.

00:10:08.666 --> 00:10:10.689
That's 55,000 times 30, carry the zeros --

00:10:10.713 --> 00:10:12.212
That's a lot of money.

00:10:12.236 --> 00:10:14.283
(Laughter)

00:10:14.815 --> 00:10:16.863
So that means,

00:10:16.887 --> 00:10:19.299
as their single and sole asset,

00:10:19.323 --> 00:10:24.189
when the time comes for them
to pass that asset on to my brother and I,

00:10:24.213 --> 00:10:28.783
that will be the first time
in my family's history,

00:10:28.807 --> 00:10:32.562
more than 150 years
after the end of slavery,

00:10:32.586 --> 00:10:36.610
that there will be a meaningful
transfer of wealth in my family.

00:10:37.745 --> 00:10:40.570
And it's not because family
members haven't saved,

00:10:40.594 --> 00:10:41.958
haven't worked hard,

00:10:41.982 --> 00:10:43.886
haven't valued education.

00:10:44.553 --> 00:10:47.612
It's because of hard history.

00:10:48.506 --> 00:10:50.467
So when I think about the past,

00:10:50.491 --> 00:10:53.426
my concern about not remembering it

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:57.654
is not that we will repeat it
if we don't remember it.

00:10:58.242 --> 00:11:02.329
My concern, my fear
is that if we don't remember the past,

00:11:02.353 --> 00:11:04.559
we will continue it.

00:11:05.186 --> 00:11:07.537
We will continue to do the things

00:11:07.561 --> 00:11:12.119
that created inequality and injustice
in the first place.

00:11:12.767 --> 00:11:14.514
So what we must do

00:11:14.538 --> 00:11:20.656
is we must disrupt
the continuum of hard history.

00:11:21.211 --> 00:11:25.225
And we can do this by seeking truth.

00:11:25.868 --> 00:11:28.725
By confronting hard history directly.

00:11:28.749 --> 00:11:33.265
By magnifying hard history
for all the world to see.

00:11:34.032 --> 00:11:37.008
We can do this by speaking truth.

00:11:37.548 --> 00:11:41.167
Teachers teaching hard history
to their students.

00:11:41.191 --> 00:11:46.018
To do anything else is to commit
educational malpractice.

00:11:46.549 --> 00:11:49.232
And parents have to speak truth
to their children,

00:11:49.256 --> 00:11:50.908
so that they understand

00:11:50.932 --> 00:11:53.986
where we have come from as a nation.

00:11:54.653 --> 00:11:58.875
And finally, we must all act on truth.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:01.844
Individually and collectively,

00:12:01.868 --> 00:12:03.622
publicly and privately,

00:12:03.646 --> 00:12:06.744
in small ways and in large ways.

00:12:06.768 --> 00:12:11.721
We must do the things that will bend
the arc of the moral universe

00:12:11.745 --> 00:12:12.966
towards justice.

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.933
To do nothing is to be complicit

00:12:16.989 --> 00:12:18.139
in inequality.

00:12:19.046 --> 00:12:21.990
History reminds us

00:12:22.014 --> 00:12:24.331
that we, as a nation,

00:12:24.355 --> 00:12:28.521
stand on the shoulders of political giants

00:12:28.545 --> 00:12:30.065
like James Madison.

00:12:30.752 --> 00:12:36.506
But hard history reminds us
that we, as a nation,

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:42.480
also stand on the shoulders
of enslaved African American children.

00:12:43.314 --> 00:12:46.759
Little Black boys and little Black girls

00:12:46.783 --> 00:12:50.862
who, with their bare hands,
made the bricks

00:12:50.886 --> 00:12:54.906
that serve as the foundation
for this nation.

00:12:55.509 --> 00:13:01.462
And if we are serious
about creating a fair and just society,

00:13:01.486 --> 00:13:05.027
then we would do well to remember that,

00:13:05.051 --> 00:13:08.198
and we would do well to remember them.

00:13:08.895 --> 00:13:10.046
Thank you.

00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:16.809
(Applause)

